              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea_and_prehistory Idea and prehistory
          %p
            It is an old result that any (finite)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph"} graph
            can be embedded in an orientable
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surface"} surface
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#White1984"} White 1984
            %em Edmonds algorithm
            and giving a date of 1960, (see
            = succeed ").)" do
              %a{:href => "#Edmonds1960"} Edmonds 1960
          %p
            In 1879
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Felix+Klein"} Felix Klein
            (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#KleinsDessins"} Klein’s dessins d’enfants
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surfaces"} Riemann surfaces
            of a certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ramification"} ramification
            type by associating to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering+space"} covers
            of the surface with certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monodromy"} monodromy
            group their “dessins d’enfants” which he called “Linienzüge”.
          %p
            The modern perception of dessins d’enfants begins with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theoretically
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            of a certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartographic+group"} cartographic group
            on sets. His motivation was the way in which this shed light on the structure of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surfaces"} Riemann surfaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolute+Galois+group"} absolute Galois group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
            %span.newWikiWord
              Belyi's theorem
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Belyi%27s+theorem"} ?
            from which follows that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            has a faithful
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            on Grothendieck’s dessins d’enfants.
          %blockquote
            %p
              A
              %em dessin d’enfant
              (French for a “child’s drawing”, plural “dessins d’enfants”, ‘’children’s drawings“) is a type of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph"} graph
              drawing used to study
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surfaces"} Riemann surfaces
              and to provide combinatorial
              %span.newWikiWord
                invariants
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Invariant+%28mathematics%29"} ?
              for the action of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolute+Galois+group"} absolute Galois group
              of the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
            %p
              “Intuitively, a dessin d’enfant is simply a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/undirected+graph"} graph
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex"} vertices
              colored alternating black and white,
              %span.newWikiWord
                embedded
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/graph+embedding"} ?
              onto an
              %span.newWikiWord
                oriented surface
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Orientability"} ?
              that, in many cases, is simply a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane"} plane
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bipartite+graph"} bipartite
              = succeed "," do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  rotation system
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/rotation+system"} ?
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cyclic+order"} cyclic order
              of the edges surrounding each vertex of the graph that describes the order in which the edges would be crossed by a path that travels clockwise on the surface in a small loop around the vertex. Any dessin can provide the surface it is embedded on with a structure as a Riemann surface. It natural to ask which Riemann surfaces arise in this way. The answer is provided by
              = succeed "," do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  Belyi's theorem
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Belyi%27s+theorem"} ?
          %p The original development does not, however, explicitly use bipartite graphs, rather it used a form of 1-complex, embedded in a surface (see below). The use of bipartite graphs simplifies the exposition.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+map"} topological map
            is an embedding of a connected graph
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            (loops and multiple edges allowed) into a compact oriented surface
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∖
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \setminus G
            is a disjoint union of open cells (referred to as the
            %em faces
            of the map). Two maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_1 , G_1 )
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_2 , G_2 )
            are called
            %em equivalent
            if there exists a homeomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X_1 \to X_2
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f (G_1 ) = G_2
            \.
          %p
            A
            %strong hypermap
            is a map together with a coloring of the vertices in black and white such that any two adjacent vertices have different colors. Observe that any map (with vertices all colored in black) gives rise to a hypermap by placing a white vertex in the middle of every edge—so, hypermaps can be seen as maps with extra information, but also conversely as a generalization of maps (see Walsh 1975, “Hypermaps Versus Bipartite Maps”).
          %p
            Finally, a
            %strong dessin d’enfant
            is a hypermap seen as a representation of a
            = succeed ":" do
              %em Belyi function
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meromorphic+function"} meromorphic function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mover
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to \bar{\mathbb{C} }
            whose only critical values are 0, 1, and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            by considering the inverse image of the unit interval
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H = f^{-1}([0,1])
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            contains exactly one
            = succeed "," do
              %em pole
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            Note that it is also possible to associate a dessin more generally to any function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mover
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to \bar{\mathbb{C}}
            with at most three critical values, since any such function may be normalized to have critical values in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0,1,\infty\}
            by applying a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+fractional+transformation"} linear fractional transformation
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/branched+cover+of+the+Riemann+sphere"} branched cover of the Riemann sphere
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Any polynomial
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mover
                        %mi ℂ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                      %mo →
                      %mover
                        %mi ℂ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : \bar{\mathbb{C}} \to \bar{\mathbb{C}}
                with no critical values other than 0 or 1 (and more generally, after normalization, any polynomial with at most two critical values) may be associated with a dessin d’enfant which is a one-face hypermap, i.e., a plane
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tree"} tree
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x) = x^n
                is the “star-tree”, containing one black vertex surrounded by
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                white vertices.
            %li
              %p
                It is well known that the bipartite graph
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi K
                        %mrow
                          %mn 3
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_{3,3}
                is non-planar, so does not embed in the 2-sphere, but will embed in the torus, in such a way that its complement is a disjoint union of discs.
          %h2#remark Remark
          %p
            The original work was stated in terms of objects called
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cellular+maps"} cellular maps
            = succeed "-sets" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔾
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{G}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔾
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{G}
            is a group called the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartographic+group"} cartographic group
          %h2#a_translated_extract A translated extract
          %p
            (from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Esquisse+d%27un+programme"} Esquisse d'un programme
            describing the idea as seen by Grothendieck:)
          %p “My interest in topological surfaces began to appear in 1974, when I proposed to Yves Ladegaillerie the theme of the isotopic study of embeddings of a topological 1-complex into a compact surface. Over the two following years, this study led him to a remarkable isotopy theorem, giving a complete algebraic description of the isotopy classes of embeddings of such 1-complexes, or compact surfaces with boundary, in a compact oriented surface, in terms of certain very simple combinatorial invariants, and the fundamental groups of the protagonists. This theorem, which should be easily generalisable to embeddings of any compact space (triangulable to simplify) in a compact oriented surface, gives as easy corollaries several deep classical results in the theory of surfaces, and in particular Baer’s isotopy theorem.
          %p In the work of Ladegaillerie there is also a purely algebraic description, in terms of fundamental groups, of the “isotopic” category of compact surfaces X , equipped with a topological 1-complex K embedded in X . This description, which had the misfortune to run counter to ‘’today’s taste’‘ and because of this appears to be unpublishable, nevertheless served (and still serves) as a precious guide in my later reflections, particularly in the context of absolute algebraic geometry in characteristic zero.“
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adinkra"} adinkra
          %h2#references References
          %p The Wikipedia page on this is:
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dessin_d'enfant"} wikipedia
          %p Fairly elementary sources for background material include:
          %ul
            %li#White1984
              Arthur T. White: 1984
              %em Graphs, groups and surfaces
          %p This gives the Edmonds algorithm for the embedding. the original source for that is:
          %ul
            %li#Edmonds1960 J. Edmonds (1960), A combinatorial representation for polyhedral surfaces, Notices Amer. Math. Soc. 7, 646.
          %p References on the deeper theory as developed by Grothendieck and his students, and including summaries of subsequent work, include:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L.+Schneps"}> L. Schneps
                , ed., 1994,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Grothendieck theory of dessins d’enfants
            %li
              %p
                Marco Robalo,
                %em Galois Theory towards Dessins d’Enfants
                (
                %a{:href => "https://dspace.ist.utl.pt/bitstream/2295/575330/1/dissertacao.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                F. Herrlich, G. Schmithüsen:
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.kit.edu/iag3/~schmithuesen/media/dessins.pdf"} Dessins d’enfants and Origami curves
          %p The original work on the elementary theory was contained in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %span.newWikiWord>
                  Christine Voisin
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Christine+Voisin"} ?
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Jean Malgoire
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Jean+Malgoire"} ?
            %li
              %p
                %span.newWikiWord>
                  Christine Voisin
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Christine+Voisin"} ?
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Jean Malgoire
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Jean+Malgoire"} ?
            %li
              %p
                %span.newWikiWord>
                  Christine Voisin
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Christine+Voisin"} ?
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Jean Malgoire
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Jean+Malgoire"} ?
          %p Other modern textbook accounts include:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Sergei K. Lando, Alexander K. Zvonkin,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Graphs on Surfaces and Their Applications
            %li
              %p
                E. Girondo, G. González-Diez,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Introduction to compact Riemann surfaces and dessins d’enfants
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 79
          %p References on the history of dessins d’enfants
          %ul
            %li
              Lieven Le Bruyn, Klein’s dessins d’enfant and the buckyball, 2008.
              %a#KleinsDessins{:href => "http://www.neverendingbooks.org/index.php/kleins-dessins-denfant-and-the-buckyball.html"} web
          %p
            Relation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix+models"} matrix models
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integrable+hierarchies"} integrable hierarchies
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jan+Ambj%C3%B8rn"}> Jan Ambjørn
              , Leonid Chekhov:
              = succeed "," do
                %em The matrix model for dessins d’enfants
              %strong 1
              3 (2014) 337-361 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1404.4240"}> arXiv:1404.4240
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.4171/aihpd/10"} doi:10.4171/aihpd/10
          %p
            On a relation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+multiplet"} super multiplet
            classification via
